just north of ontario's largest city  sets an equestrian oasis a little farm a  stone's throw from lake simcoe  it's here diane van kulin finds comfort  and an escape from what she's facing  the 61 year old has stage 4 lung cancer  i remember seeing the x-ray on the on  the screen in the er room thinking that  better not be mine  being diagnosed with stage 4 lung cancer  was such an overwhelming diagnosis it  took me time to process it  the cancer had spread to her lymph nodes  ribs and spine  she was getting treatment and then the  pandemic hit  diane started feeling sicker and sicker  but the idea of going to a hospital for  care  the fear of getting kovid 19 it was too  much  she delayed going and unbeknownst to her  the cancer treatment  wasn't working the result some of  diane's  tumors tripled in size i was my own  worst enemy i refused to go in for four  weeks and my body became  more broken what would have happened if  i went in  at the beginning would i be at less risk  of progression  diane's story and stories like hers have  doctors deeply concerned  all aspects of cancer care from  diagnosis to treatment  are being impacted by covet 19. when you  say that kovit has had a profound  implication on the delivery of cancer  care  this is big dr lucy gilbert is the  director of gynecological oncology at  mcgill university health center in  montreal  she says one of the harshest realities  of the pandemic  finding out you have cancer then being  told your care might be postponed  it's heartbreaking and you wonder  do i want to continue in this position  because in a way i feel you are  party to a crime  we are the ones who will deal with the  patients  who are petrified who are afraid who  know cancer will not wait  according to the canadian institute for  health information from march to june  2020  cancer surgeries were down 20 across the  country  some hospitals were able to catch up but  the third wave has put  all that in jeopardy many surgeries even  cancer surgeries deemed urgent have once  again  been put on hold and some oncologists  like dr gilbert  have to meet with committees every week  to decide who's sick enough to get a  surgical spot  we have to present the patients who we  think are  very sick and who if we do not give them  a date within three to four weeks  will come to severe harm it's almost  a bit like a lottery i am competing  with say the vascular surgeons who say  the patient may lose a limb or the eye  surgeon who says the patient will become  blind and that's not a way  we want to run a health care system  but this is what we do now  how many surgeries have been postponed  would you say  yeah it's in the thousands if we're  talking about all surgeries  it's gonna soon hit over a hundred  thousand like it's like it's a  astronomical number dr antoine iskander  is a surgical oncologist at sunnybrook  health sciences center in toronto  he's been collecting data on new  diagnoses oncology surgeries  and screening tests being done during  the pandemic  we saw a 60 drop in cancer directed  surgeries  on the week of march 15 of 2020 in  ontario  and subsequent to that over the next few  months we had an increase in the amount  of surgery but we never hit  pre-pandemic rates of cancer surgery  but the true magnitude of the problem  won't be told for some  time it'll take a year two years because  there's patients we haven't even heard  about  that should be in the system but aren't  even on the wait list yet  that's because some people are not  getting screened so many would-be cancer  patients  haven't been diagnosed yet you wonder  where some of these cancers are  some of them are missing in action we  don't know where they are we've seen a  drop  in cancer incidence rates despite  there being a very steady rate of  cancers being diagnosed in ontario over  the last 10 years  and that's very worrisome physicians say  when people  finally seek care their cancers are far  worse than if they had come in when they  first had symptoms  if you get these cancers in stage one  the cure rate  exceeds 1995  but we are seeing much more patients  in what we call advanced unresectable  stages  and once it becomes stage four its  cure is almost impossible  a recent u.s study found nearly 10  million cancer screenings failed to  happen in 2020  alone because of covin here at home  data is still being collected so it's  impossible to know how many people will  be impacted  people are so frightened of going  and seeing doctors the likelihood of  catching covet from a health care  facilities  is minuscule because we are obsessional  about following their rules  so please please report problems in time  have your tests in time  for some canadians who are sick enough  to get immediate cancer care during the  pandemic  there have been tough decisions  [Music]  more than a year ago don pickering's  seven-year-old son oliver  had what looked like a small bump on his  neck it turned out to be anaplastic  large cell lymphoma  a rare form of blood cancer to survive  oliver needed a stem cell transplant his  health team  in ottawa turned to the donation  registry for help  so you can imagine you know our shock  and our concern  but they found three perfect matches so  we really felt like we had won the  lottery which was pretty incredible  sadly the relief was short-lived at this  time the pandemic was declared and the  country went into lockdown  oliver's matches were international and  because of travel restrictions and covet  19 concerns  they weren't able to donate he just  moved his fingers  with limited options the family had to  ask their 11 year old daughter abby who  was a half match  and then i came home and then my mom was  there  and she goes so we have some news  and then at that moment i was just like  oh my goodness oh my goodness oh my  goodness  and then she told me that we needed  knee for the stem cells and i was like  okay well i just thought of all the  scenarios and i thought like my brother  was gonna die  so that's better than him dying at least  he has a chance to live  [Music]  when we found out that we were going to  have to use our daughter's stem cells  for me that certainly stressed me out  quite a bit  because now i have not just one sick  child but a second child who i have to  make sure is protected  and that nothing happens to her and she  doesn't get covered  it was a risk the family was forced to  take i  still can't believe that i'm gonna do it  then i'm going to do stem cells  after many painful procedures and lots  of covered safety precautions  abby was able to donate you know  we'll get through this and it'll be okay  so i'm just going to stay here  i was like if this was ollie he would  definitely do it for me because my  brother is a very  kind compassionate empathetic guy and  he's  always been there for me your sister  she was the one who gave you her stem  cells how did that feel  she was really kind to me and now since  i'm well we go back to  brother and sister sibling fights  okay ready rock paper scissors shoot  [Laughter]  how is oliver doing now he's doing  fantastic actually i'm really  pleased to tell you that he's very well  daily he shocks us with what he can do  and  how quickly he manages to to recover  from all the things that have happened  to you  laser laser cuts rock in half  oliver was one of the lucky ones others  haven't been so fortunate  experts say with the current state of  cancer care we're just trading one  health crisis  for another it's been described as  there's going to be a tsunami of cancer  cases after we get out of this copic  crisis  i don't disagree with that i think there  will be an uptick  of identifying these patients that have  not been diagnosed yet  but the question is are we going to  identify them at a point where there's  very little we can do  that's what scares us i would gladly  take a tsunami  of patients that needed treatment and  sorting out how we would do that  over patients who we've missed who now  can't get treatment  and we just don't know yet how that's  going to pan out  it's so interesting that you're saying a  backlogger tsunami would be actually a  good thing  compared to what you think could  potentially happen exactly  i would almost welcome a tsunami because  i think we would find a way  to make it work we'd have to be very  creative and very  thoughtful and we'd have to try a few  new things  but we would probably find a way to make  it work  as for diane van kulin she's had several  rounds of treatment and is now  on a new cancer drug i hope this drug  just keeps working for a long long time  because i have a lot of things i want to  do still  in my life so far she's feeling stronger  she's even gotten back up on her horse  but diane hopes her story can serve as a  cautionary tale for those  avoiding care i'm trying to be  respectful of people's  fear right but what i've learned is fear  gets you nowhere  and if you have that opportunity to go  to an er  if you have the opportunity to get  treated that day  don't wait four weeks cancer can grow  you know you've got to weigh the  benefits the pros and cons and they're  definitely in my case were far more  cons for waiting as long as i did learn  my lesson
 

